    fix(lang): SOL-R6 — provable domain inverts to a whitelist via a three-state machine
    
    Third widening round, so the shape inverts per arbiter ruling: the
    refused-substring blacklist becomes a WHITELIST enforced by a minimal
    state machine (code / plain_string / line_comment). Provable domain,
    stated in the module docstring: LF-only documents; plain strings whose
    only escapes are \\ and \" and which never span a newline; // line
    comments (content inert); printable-ASCII code (plus \n/\t).
    Everything else refuses at first occurrence: non-LF newlines anywhere
    (R6-01 — \r \f U+0085 U+2028 U+2029 desync line accounting), /* block
    comments and /- slashdash in code (R6-02 — a quote inside /* " */
    poisoned flat tracking, letting a following r" masquerade as a closing
    quote), raw-string delimiters of any hash depth (r-before-opening-quote
    is trivially position-aware in the machine — only CODE state refuses),
    multi-line strings, non-whitelisted escapes (arbiter addendum a; corpus
    has ZERO backslashes of any kind), non-ASCII/control chars in code
    state (addendum b — BOM/lookalikes wholesale; legal inside strings and
    comments), unterminated strings. /* INSIDE a plain string stays legal —
    glob patterns like "./**/*.loop" are real corpus fixtures, which is
    why this is a machine, not substring search. Domain check now runs
    BEFORE the parse in all three verbs (pure text precondition). Not the
    KDL lexer we declined: the smallest machine whose states make the
    whitelist provable.
    
    Regressions (red-first vs the r5 flat scan: 18 failures): sol's two r6
    repro classes (5 non-LF newline forms; block-comment quote concealment)
    plus plain block comment, slashdash, 4 refused escape forms, 6
    non-ASCII/control code-position chars. Pins kept: multi-hash 1-8,
    r-before-closing-quote inside strings, glob-in-string, quote-inside-//
    now inert (the flat scan wrongly refused it), whitelisted escapes,
    non-ASCII inside strings, full corpus round-trip.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
